= Sources =

'''A source is an identifiable origin, record or transmission of information.''' A citation makes it possible to locate that information; a source assessment asks whether it is authentic, appropriately interpreted, and relevant to the claim being made. The same source may be highly informative about one question and inadequate for another.

Sources are not the same as [[BITwiki:Evidence|evidence]]. A source tells us where information comes from and how it reached us. Evidence is the information's bearing on a specific proposition. Neither a prestigious publication nor a digital signature can substitute for evaluating that relationship.

== Kinds of sources ==

=== Original or primary material ===

Original experimental reports, datasets, first-hand accounts, code, official records, correspondence, technical specifications and contemporaneous documents provide close access to a particular event, observation or statement.

'''Primary''' is relative to the question. A research paper is a primary report of its own experiment; the same paper may be a secondary account of a historical theory it summarizes. Primary material may also contain error, bias, omissions or unsupported interpretations.

=== Synthesis and secondary analysis ===

Reviews, meta-analyses, critical studies, systematic comparisons and interpretive scholarship connect and evaluate earlier work. A rigorous synthesis may offer a stronger view of a body of research than any one study, provided its search, selection, methods and limitations are transparent.

Distinguish a systematic review from an informal narrative review, and a review's findings from its authors' conclusions. Reporting guidelines such as [https://www.prisma-statement.org/prisma-2020-statement PRISMA 2020] improve transparency about how systematic reviews were conducted; following a reporting checklist does not itself guarantee the review's validity.

=== Reference and orientation material ===

Encyclopedias, handbooks, textbooks, glossaries, bibliographies and other reference works can establish terminology, introduce a field and direct readers toward foundational research. They may be excellent introductory sources without being sufficient evidence for disputed or newly emerging claims.

=== Community, practitioner and institutional material ===

Developer discussions, project issue trackers, professional reports, technical forums, community observations, industry documentation, government communications and advocacy publications can provide direct evidence of practice, statements, decisions or operational behavior.

Their authority is specific. An official announcement establishes that an institution announced something; it does not automatically establish that the institution's prediction or substantive claim is correct. Where possible, test operational claims against actual implementation or independently observed behavior.

=== Records with integrity protections ===

Signed statements, cryptographically verifiable records, audit logs and timestamped datasets may allow stronger checks of authorship, integrity and transmission. Such protections do not turn a false or misleading statement into a true one.

These descriptions overlap. They are aids to investigation, not tiers of truth.

== Questions for evaluating a source ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Consideration !! What to establish
|-
| '''Identity''' || Who created, published or maintained this specific record? Can its author and origin be established?
|-
| '''Authenticity''' || Is the artifact genuine, complete and unaltered in ways that affect the claim?
|-
| '''Method''' || How was the information collected, measured, inferred, checked or compiled?
|-
| '''Proximity''' || Is this firsthand information, an interpretation or a repetition of another source?
|-
| '''Competence''' || What relevant access, expertise or firsthand knowledge does the author possess, and where are its limits?
|-
| '''Independence''' || Does this source provide an additional observation, or does it repeat the same original claim?
|-
| '''Interests and incentives''' || What relevant funding, commercial, ideological or institutional interests should be disclosed and examined?
|-
| '''Currency and version''' || Is the record appropriate to the time, software release or historical period being discussed? Has it been corrected, updated or retracted?
|-
| '''Context and scope''' || Which part of the assertion does the source actually establish, and which part remains interpretation?
|}

These considerations should be proportionate to the claim and the consequences of error. Reputation and credentials can be relevant signals, but not conclusive substitutes for methods, access, and supporting material.

== Provenance and transmission ==

Information may travel through multiple intermediaries:

<pre>
original observation or work
   → report or publication
   → review or summary
   → quotation or adaptation
   → BITwiki article
</pre>

At each step, wording, context, uncertainty and attribution can change. A quotation can be accurate but misleading when separated from its conditions; several citations can trace back to a single unsupported source.

Where a claim is consequential, trace it as close to its origin as practical. Distinguish a source-authored statement from an editor's inference or synthesis. Record material transformations without treating every intermediary as independent confirmation. See [[BITwiki:Provenance]].

== Conflicts, criticism and correction ==

Financial backing, employment, advocacy or professional interests may influence a source's choices of questions, measurements or presentation. Disclosure permits examination; a conflict is not by itself proof of falsehood, nor is declared independence proof of accuracy. Relevant guidance on disclosure appears in the [https://www.icmje.org/recommendations/browse/roles-and-responsibilities/author-responsibilities--conflicts-of-interest.html International Committee of Medical Journal Editors recommendations].

Inspect corrections, retractions, changed definitions, disputed interpretations and failed replication attempts where material. A source may remain important as historical evidence even when its factual conclusion has been superseded.

== Citation and verification ==

A useful reference identifies the '''specific''' work, not merely a homepage or an authority's name. Include as appropriate: author or issuing organization, title, publication date, version or revision, publisher or venue, stable identifier (such as DOI), URL or archive location, and the page, section, table, figure or code revision supporting the assertion.

Before relying on a citation:

# Confirm that the referenced artifact exists and is the intended version.
# Locate the specific passage, dataset or behavior being cited.
# Check whether it supports the wording and scope of the article's claim.
# Consult original records or independent corroboration when the stakes or controversy warrant it.
# Update or qualify a claim when its source is corrected, contradicted, retracted or obsolete.

The strength of a reference lies in what a reader can check, not the number of citations attached to a sentence.

== Sources created or transformed by AI ==

Generated prose, synthesized summaries and machine-produced bibliographies can contain invented references or real references attached to claims those sources do not support. Their fluent presentation is not independent evidence. NIST identifies such confabulations and fabricated citations as a generative-AI risk in its [https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence Generative AI Profile].

Machine assistance can help find and organize literature, compare texts or identify missing provenance. Check the original materials independently before using them to support factual conclusions. Where machine transformation materially affects the resulting account, distinguish the source-authored material from generated inference and editorial judgment.

== Sources in BITwiki ==

We aim to preserve the connection between a public claim and the material that allows it to be examined. Sources should be chosen for their bearing on that claim, not their prestige, popularity or compatibility with an established narrative. Where evidence is contested, represent the meaningful disagreement and its strongest support instead of using selective quotation to imply certainty.

See [[BITwiki:Evidence]] for evaluating what a source establishes, [[BITwiki:Claim validation]] for reviewing a particular claim, [[BITwiki:Provenance]] for tracing transmission, and [[BITwiki:Uncertainty]] for communicating limits.

